1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,016
Where may a card number live in your system?

2
00:00:02,066 --> 00:00:07,296
Picture a shop that keeps card numbers in its orders table, just in case it needs them for refunds.

3
00:00:07,346 --> 00:00:12,048
A card number is dangerous to keep, because anyone who has one can try to pay with it.

4
00:00:12,098 --> 00:00:13,920
Then the numbers spread.

5
00:00:13,970 --> 00:00:16,968
A report reads the orders table every morning.

6
00:00:17,018 --> 00:00:19,344
A backup copies it every night.

7
00:00:19,394 --> 00:00:22,056
And every developer's laptop can reach it.

8
00:00:22,106 --> 00:00:29,688
Now each of those systems holds card numbers, so each of them has to be checked: its network, its logins, its logs and its changes.

9
00:00:29,738 --> 00:00:33,624
Even a simple fee change now waits for a card data review.

10
00:00:33,674 --> 00:00:38,472
And it only takes one weak laptop to leak every card the shop has ever seen.

11
00:00:38,522 --> 00:00:43,536
Every new copy is one more place to protect, and one more place to forget.

12
00:00:43,586 --> 00:00:45,648
Those checks come from one rulebook.

13
00:00:45,698 --> 00:00:53,664
P C I D S S is the card industry's security rulebook, and it checks every system that touches a card number, or can reach one.

14
00:00:53,714 --> 00:00:57,600
So the way out is not to check everything, but to have less to check.

15
00:00:57,650 --> 00:00:59,472
Think of a coat check.

16
00:00:59,522 --> 00:01:03,432
Coats go into one locked room, and everyone else gets a ticket.

17
00:01:03,482 --> 00:01:08,712
With the ticket you can get your coat back, but the ticket itself is worth nothing to a thief.

18
00:01:08,762 --> 00:01:11,448
Only the attendant ever touches the coats.

19
00:01:11,498 --> 00:01:20,664
Card numbers work the same way: they go into one locked room, and every other system gets a ticket, the card token from lesson three, never the card number.

20
00:01:20,714 --> 00:01:23,280
Now only the room needs the full check.

21
00:01:23,330 --> 00:01:28,704
In this system, the locked room is the cards unit, and this course calls it the card zone.

22
00:01:28,754 --> 00:01:33,192
The card zone has its own database, its own keys, and its own logins.

23
00:01:33,242 --> 00:01:38,136
It shares none of them with ecom, because anything the room shared would need the full check too.

24
00:01:38,186 --> 00:01:40,224
The room has only two doors.

25
00:01:40,274 --> 00:01:43,584
One is for payers' browsers, which load the card form.

26
00:01:43,634 --> 00:01:46,656
The other is for partner systems, like ecom.

27
00:01:46,706 --> 00:01:52,176
When a payer types a card, it goes straight through the first door into the room, never through ecom.

28
00:01:52,226 --> 00:01:57,216
Nothing else gets in, and the database inside cannot be reached from outside at all.

29
00:01:57,266 --> 00:01:59,496
ecom only ever holds tickets.

30
00:01:59,546 --> 00:02:04,632
It asks the card zone to charge a card by its token, and the card number never leaves the room.

31
00:02:04,682 --> 00:02:11,160
ecom's database, reports, backups and laptops hold tickets too, so a leak there leaks no card.

32
00:02:11,210 --> 00:02:17,184
Even ecom's developers can follow a payment from end to end without ever seeing a card number.

33
00:02:17,234 --> 00:02:19,032
Here is the honest part.

34
00:02:19,082 --> 00:02:21,744
ecom still gets a check, just a smaller one.

35
00:02:21,794 --> 00:02:28,512
It shows the card form on its payment page, and it takes payments with tickets, so the rulebook still cares about it.

36
00:02:28,562 --> 00:02:30,192
Smaller, not none.

37
00:02:30,242 --> 00:02:36,768
Lesson five draws that line exactly: what ecom may hold, and what it may ask the card zone for.

38
00:02:36,818 --> 00:02:38,904
Now check the room on your own laptop.

39
00:02:38,954 --> 00:02:44,256
With both units running from lesson one, ask Docker Compose what the card zone publishes.

40
00:02:44,306 --> 00:02:47,856
The command lists each service with the ports it opens on your machine.

41
00:02:47,906 --> 00:02:54,000
Two doors are open, cards ingress and cards partner, and Postgres has no port on the outside at all.

42
00:02:54,050 --> 00:02:59,736
So card numbers live in one place, everything else holds tokens, and the check shrinks to fit.

43
00:02:59,786 --> 00:03:07,152
In a bank, the card zone runs inside the bank's own secured network, with the bank's guards, firewalls and checks around it.

44
00:03:07,202 --> 00:03:10,464
We deliver the locked room; the bank owns the building.

45
00:03:10,514 --> 00:03:14,112
Next: the two units, and the contract between them.
